.TH TIMECLOCK 1
.SH NAME
timeclock \- punch clock with weekly totals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/timeclock
[
.I in
|
.I out
|
.I rep
[
.I description ...
] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I TimeClock
is a port of the TempleOS
.B Apps/TimeClock
punch clock.
With no arguments it opens a window under
.IR wm (1);
with an argument it does the one thing and exits, as the original's
console commands do.
.PP
Each punch records the time and a description.
The report lists every entry and, following
.IR TimeRep ,
groups them by week \- the day number divided by seven \- printing a
.B "Week Total"
whenever the week changes and again at the end.
A shift still open counts up to the present.
.PP
Punching in when already in, or out when already out, is refused with
a blinking warning, as the original's
.B $$BK,1$$
text does.
.PP
In the window, type the description first and then press
.B "Punch In"
or
.BR "Punch Out" .
On the command line the description may be given as arguments; if it
is not, it is read from standard input after the punch, as
.I GetStr
does in the original, ending at a line containing only a dot.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF "Punch In / Punch Out"
.TP
.B "Punch In / Punch Out"
Record a punch with the typed description
.TP
.B Report
Redraw the report
.TP
.B Esc / q
Quit
.PD
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/$user/timeclock/timefile
The log: one record a line, holding the punch type, the time and the
escaped description.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/timeclock.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR daytime (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The original stores binary
.B CDate
records, compressed, in
.BR ~/TimeClock/TimeFile.DATA.Z ;
this stores uncompressed text, so the two files are not interchangeable.
In the window the description is typed before the punch rather than
prompted for after it.
.PP
End of file used to be listed here as a bug: a punch whose description
came from a redirected standard input had to end with a dot or it waited
forever.
.I Readdesc
stopped when the read returned zero, which was right, but under
.IR emu (1)
that never happened \- host standard input reaching end of file killed the
keyboard slave without telling the console queue, so any reader of
.B /dev/cons
blocked.
That is fixed in
.IR emu (1)
itself, and the same hang went with it for every other command in this
tree that reads all of standard input.
